<DocsLayout
  title="Client"
  subtitle="Drive a running nikcli server from your own program with the generated, typed HTTP client."
  description="Using @nikcli-ai/sdk to drive a nikcli server: creating a client, the result envelope, instance selection, streaming, authentication and errors."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="overview" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      <code>@nikcli-ai/sdk</code> wraps the server's HTTP API in a typed, namespaced client. It is
      generated from the same OpenAPI document as the <a href="/docs/api">API reference</a> by
      <code>@nikcli-ai/httpapi-codegen</code>, so every endpoint is a method and a contract change
      breaks generation rather than becoming a runtime 404.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the path the web app, the desktop app and the mobile app already take. Nothing about
      them is privileged — they call the endpoints you can call.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
npm install @nikcli-ai/sdk
    </DocCode>
    <p>You also need a server to talk to:</p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
nikcli serve --hostname 127.0.0.1 --port 4096
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="create" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Create a client</h2>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`import { createNikcliClient } from "@nikcli-ai/sdk/httpapi"

const client = createNikcliClient({
  baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:4096",
  // binds every call to one project instance
  directory: "/Users/me/project",
})`}</DocCode>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Type</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>baseUrl</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td>Required. Where the server is listening.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>directory</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td>Sent as <code>x-nikcli-directory</code> on every call. Non-ASCII paths are percent-encoded for you.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>workspace</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td>Sent as <code>x-nikcli-workspace</code> on every call.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>headers</code></td><td><code>HeadersInit</code></td><td>Merged into every request — this is where an <code>Authorization</code> header goes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>signal</code></td><td><code>AbortSignal</code></td><td>Applied to every request, combined with any per-call signal.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>fetch</code></td><td><code>typeof fetch</code></td><td>Swap the transport — a proxy, a mock, or an in-memory handler.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>throwOnError</code></td><td><code>boolean</code></td><td>Reject on failure instead of resolving with <code>error</code>. See below.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="results" class="doc-section">
    <h2>The result envelope</h2>
    <p>
      By default calls <strong>resolve</strong> with an envelope rather than rejecting. Transport
      failures and declared HTTP errors both arrive as <code>error</code>, so a failed call in a UI
      event handler cannot become an unhandled rejection.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const { data, error } = await client.session.list()
// guard on \`data\`, not on \`error\`: \`error\` is \`unknown\`, so testing it
// does not narrow \`data\` away from \`undefined\`
if (!data) throw error

data.forEach((session) => console.log(session.id, session.title))`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      In a script, where an unhandled rejection is exactly what you want, opt back into throwing —
      per call or once for the whole client:
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const client = createNikcliClient({ baseUrl, throwOnError: true })

// rejects on failure, so \`data\` is always present at runtime
const { data } = await client.session.list()

// or per call, on a client that does not throw by default
const one = await client.session.get({ sessionID }, { throwOnError: true })`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="throwOnError does not change the type">
      <p>
        It changes runtime behaviour only. The call still returns
        <code>{"{ data, error }"}</code> and <code>data</code> is still typed as possibly
        <code>undefined</code>, so you keep destructuring it — with <code>data!</code>, or a guard,
        or a one-line <code>unwrap</code> helper of your own.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="instance" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Choosing an instance</h2>
    <p>
      One server hosts many projects. The client's <code>directory</code> is the default for every
      call, and any call can override it — useful when one process drives several checkouts.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`// the client default
await client.session.list()

// inline, for one call
await client.session.list({ directory: "/Users/me/other-project" })

// a workspace within the instance
await client.session.list({ workspace: workspaceID })`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      The server resolves this exactly as described in
      <a href="/docs/api#instance">Choosing an instance</a>: the header the client sets is the same
      one <code>curl</code> would send.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="methods" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Method names</h2>
    <p>
      Methods are namespaced by resource and follow the operation ids listed throughout the
      <a href="/docs/api">API reference</a>: <code>session.prompt</code> is
      <code>client.session.prompt</code>, <code>mobile.git.commit</code> is
      <code>client.mobile.git.commit</code>. A handful are renamed where the dotted id would collide
      with a JavaScript keyword or an existing namespace — <code>session.messageRemove</code> is
      <code>client.session.messageDelete</code>, for instance — so let the types lead rather than
      transcribing ids by hand.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`await client.session.create({ title: "triage" })
await client.session.messages({ sessionID })
await client.file.read({ path: "src/index.ts" })
await client.find.text({ pattern: "TODO" })
await client.project.current()
await client.config.get()
await client.doctor.run()`}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="session" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Running a session</h2>
    <p>
      The common shape: create a session, send a prompt, read what came back. Everything else —
      sharing, forking, reverting, todos, diffs — hangs off the same session id.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="triage.ts">{`import { createNikcliClient } from "@nikcli-ai/sdk/httpapi"

const client = createNikcliClient({
  baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:4096",
  directory: process.cwd(),
  throwOnError: true,
})

const { data: session } = await client.session.create({ title: "triage" })

const { data: reply } = await client.session.prompt({
  sessionID: session!.id,
  parts: [{ type: "text", text: "Which tests are currently failing, and why?" }],
})

for (const part of reply!.parts) {
  if (part.type === "text") console.log(part.text)
}

const { data: diff } = await client.session.diff({ sessionID: session!.id })
console.log(diff)`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>session.prompt</code> waits for the assistant to finish. For fire-and-forget submission
      — queue the work and watch the event feed instead — use
      <code>client.session.promptAsync</code>.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="Pick the model at the session, not the call">
      <p>
        <code>session.prompt</code> accepts an explicit <code>model</code> and <code>agent</code>,
        but leaving them unset inherits the launching session's model, which is usually what you
        want for background work.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="streams" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Streams and events</h2>
    <p>
      Streaming endpoints resolve to an object holding an async iterable, so you await the
      connection and then iterate the feed.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const { stream } = await client.event.subscribe()

for await (const event of stream) {
  if (event.type === "message.part.updated" && event.properties.part.type === "text") {
    // \`delta\` is the text added since the last event; \`part.text\` is cumulative
    process.stdout.write(event.properties.delta ?? "")
  }
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>client.event.subscribe()</code> is scoped to the resolved instance;
      <code>client.global.event()</code> spans every instance the server hosts, and
      <code>client.sync.event.stream()</code> carries sync traffic. All three are the SSE feeds
      listed under <a href="/docs/api#streaming">Streaming</a>.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Pair promptAsync with the feed">
      <p>
        <code>session.promptAsync</code> returns as soon as the work is accepted. Subscribe first,
        then submit, and you see the assistant's output as it is produced instead of waiting for the
        whole turn. <a href="/docs/guides/streaming">Streaming a response</a> walks the whole flow.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="auth" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Authentication</h2>
    <p>
      On loopback with no password set, nothing is needed. Anything else goes through
      <code>headers</code> — the client does not manage credentials for you.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`// bearer token (mobile pairing token or an nku_ account token)
const client = createNikcliClient({
  baseUrl,
  headers: { Authorization: \`Bearer \${process.env.NIKCLI_TOKEN}\` },
})

// basic auth, for a server started with NIKCLI_SERVER_PASSWORD
const basic = btoa(\`nikcli:\${process.env.NIKCLI_SERVER_PASSWORD}\`)
const client2 = createNikcliClient({
  baseUrl,
  headers: { Authorization: \`Basic \${basic}\` },
})`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      The full scheme table is in <a href="/docs/api#auth">Authentication</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="errors" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Errors</h2>
    <p>
      <code>ClientError</code> is what a transport or protocol failure resolves to. It folds in its
      cause, so a server that died mid-request reports why rather than a bare
      <code>Transport</code>.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`import { ClientError, createNikcliClient } from "@nikcli-ai/sdk/httpapi"

const { data, error, response } = await client.session.get({ sessionID })

if (error instanceof ClientError) {
  console.error(error.reason, error.message, error.cause)
} else if (error) {
  // a declared HTTP error — the schema named on the endpoint in the API reference
  console.error(response?.status, error)
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      Declared failures are typed on the endpoint, so a <code>ConfigUpdateError</code> or a
      <code>ProviderAuthError</code> arrives as that shape rather than collapsing into a generic
      500. The endpoint pages list which errors each operation can return.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="other-languages" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Other languages</h2>
    <p>
      There is no official client beyond JavaScript, but the contract is public. Point any OpenAPI
      generator at the document and you get the same surface.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
curl -s https://nikcli.store/docs/api/openapi.json -o nikcli-openapi.json
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      Operation ids are stable and dotted (<code>session.prompt</code>,
      <code>experimental.workspace.warp</code>), so most generators produce sensibly grouped method
      names without extra configuration.
    </p>
  </section>

